The Orca research system described iteration-level scheduling, NVIDIA TensorRT-LLM supports in-flight batching, and vLLM supports continuous batching. They are different systems, but the shared mechanism is simple: do not freeze one group until every answer ends.

Continuous batching reduces empty seats, but it does not erase tradeoffs. Every active request needs KV-cache memory. A very large batch can run out of cache capacity, lengthen each generation step or force the scheduler to pause work. A long prompt entering prefill can also delay active decode requests unless the software splits or prioritizes that work carefully.

Why latency rises near saturation

Four measurements tell different stories. Time to first token includes queueing and prompt processing. Time between tokens measures the pace of the visible answer. End-to-end latency measures the whole request. Throughput measures total output across all users.

When demand approaches the server's maximum sustainable throughput, even a small burst can build a queue faster than the accelerator clears it. Utilization may look excellent while waiting time rises sharply. Operators therefore need spare capacity, admission limits or more replicas if they promise a latency target. Keeping every accelerator full at all times is not automatically the right objective.

Prediction check

Two configurations use the same model and hardware. Configuration A keeps batches small. Configuration B waits longer and serves larger batches. Which one is likely to have lower cost per token, and which one is likely to begin an individual answer sooner?

Answer: B will often have lower cost per token because it completes more total work with the same hardware. A will often begin an answer sooner because it adds less batching and queue delay. The result can reverse if A wastes so much hardware that requests form a long backlog, which is why operators measure the complete system rather than assuming one batch size is always best.

What this means across the stack

Better scheduling can turn the same accelerators, HBM and power into more useful output, so software changes the amount of physical infrastructure needed for a given traffic load. But higher utilization can also worsen the user's experience if the service optimizes only total tokens per second.

For an operator or investor, the useful question is not simply whether accelerators are busy. Ask what latency target the service promises, how traffic arrives, how much KV-cache memory requests consume, what scheduling policy is used and how throughput behaves at the target latency. Google Cloud describes this as an efficient frontier: for a fixed hardware budget, the operator chooses among different combinations of latency and throughput.

